WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.521
A California county changes the text message sent before public-benefit appointments.

2
00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:06.861
The next month, missed appointments decline.

3
00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:09.733
A manager says the new wording caused the improvement.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:20.104
Psychology gives us tools to ask a better question: what evidence would justify that causal claim, and what does the message ask a person's attention, learning, memory, and social judgment to do?

5
00:00:20.104 --> 00:00:21.627
This is preparation only.

6
00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:25.586
This episode does not grant, predict, or transfer college credit.

7
00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:28.681
Psychology is the scientific study of mind and behavior.

8
00:00:28.681 --> 00:00:31.057
Scientific does not mean perfectly certain.

9
00:00:31.057 --> 00:00:36.750
It means claims are tied to observable evidence, methods are described, and explanations can be tested.

10
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.734
A theory organizes findings and generates predictions.

11
00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.387
A hypothesis is a specific, testable prediction.

12
00:00:42.387 --> 00:00:47.084
Operational definitions state exactly how a variable will be measured or manipulated.

13
00:00:47.084 --> 00:00:51.063
If a team says a message improved engagement, it must define engagement.

14
00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:56.203
Did the resident open the message, click a link, arrive on time, or complete the appointment?

15
00:00:56.203 --> 00:00:58.634
Research designs answer different questions.

16
00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:01.564
Observation records behavior in its ordinary setting.

17
00:01:01.564 --> 00:01:03.774
Surveys report experiences or attitudes.

18
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:06.095
Case studies examine one situation deeply.

19
00:01:06.095 --> 00:01:10.461
These approaches can identify patterns, but usually cannot establish causation.

20
00:01:10.461 --> 00:01:14.109
Correlation measures the direction and strength of an association.

21
00:01:14.109 --> 00:01:19.248
Positive correlations move together, while negative correlations move in opposite directions.

22
00:01:19.248 --> 00:01:25.438
Correlation does not prove causation because a third variable may affect both, or the direction may be reversed.

23
00:01:25.438 --> 00:01:30.080
An experiment manipulates an independent variable and measures a dependent variable.

24
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.336
Random assignment helps distribute preexisting differences across conditions.

25
00:01:34.336 --> 00:01:39.862
An experimental group receives the condition of interest, and a control group provides a comparison.

26
00:01:39.862 --> 00:01:42.018
Blinding can reduce expectancy effects.

27
00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:44.781
Replication tests whether a finding appears again.

28
00:01:44.781 --> 00:01:53.347
Researchers also use descriptive and inferential statistics to summarize data and judge whether a result is likely to reflect more than sampling variation.

29
00:01:53.347 --> 00:01:54.784
Ethics are part of method.

30
00:01:54.784 --> 00:02:02.134
Human research requires informed consent where applicable, protection from unnecessary harm, privacy, and an opportunity to withdraw.

31
00:02:02.134 --> 00:02:05.008
Deception needs strong justification and debriefing.

32
00:02:05.008 --> 00:02:08.324
Public agencies also face legal duties and power imbalances.

33
00:02:08.324 --> 00:02:12.855
A resident should not believe benefits depend on joining a study when they do not.

34
00:02:12.855 --> 00:02:17.111
Learning is a lasting change in behavior or knowledge produced by experience.

35
00:02:17.111 --> 00:02:21.366
In classical conditioning, an organism learns an association between stimuli.

36
00:02:21.366 --> 00:02:27.114
A neutral cue can come to trigger a response after repeated pairing with a cue that already triggers it.

37
00:02:27.114 --> 00:02:29.048
Acquisition builds the association.

38
00:02:29.048 --> 00:02:33.138
Extinction weakens a conditioned response when the expected pairing stops.

39
00:02:33.138 --> 00:02:37.725
Spontaneous recovery is the reappearance of an extinguished response after a pause.

40
00:02:37.725 --> 00:02:43.085
Generalization spreads responding to similar cues, while discrimination distinguishes among them.

41
00:02:43.085 --> 00:02:47.341
Operant conditioning concerns associations between behavior and consequences.

42
00:02:47.341 --> 00:02:49.275
Reinforcement increases a behavior.

43
00:02:49.275 --> 00:02:50.601
Punishment decreases it.

44
00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:54.525
Positive means adding something, and negative means removing something.

45
00:02:54.525 --> 00:02:56.570
Those words do not mean good and bad.

46
00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:02.318
A fee waived after on-time filing is negative reinforcement if removing the fee increases timely filing.

47
00:03:02.318 --> 00:03:06.241
A late penalty is positive punishment if adding it reduces late filing.

48
00:03:06.241 --> 00:03:09.060
The actual effect on behavior determines the label.

49
00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.321
Continuous reinforcement rewards every qualifying response.

50
00:03:12.321 --> 00:03:16.355
Partial reinforcement rewards some responses and can produce persistence.

51
00:03:16.355 --> 00:03:19.229
Shaping reinforces successive steps toward a target.

52
00:03:19.229 --> 00:03:22.876
Observational learning occurs by watching models and consequences.

53
00:03:22.876 --> 00:03:27.463
Cognition includes thinking, language, concepts, problem solving, and intelligence.

54
00:03:27.463 --> 00:03:29.121
Concepts organize information.

55
00:03:29.121 --> 00:03:32.382
Prototypes are typical examples used to classify new cases.

56
00:03:32.382 --> 00:03:35.808
Algorithms are step-by-step methods that can yield a solution.

57
00:03:35.808 --> 00:03:39.842
Heuristics are mental shortcuts that save effort but can introduce error.

58
00:03:39.842 --> 00:03:43.987
The availability heuristic makes easily recalled examples feel more common.

59
00:03:43.987 --> 00:03:47.690
Confirmation bias favors evidence that supports an existing belief.

60
00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:51.946
Fixation can keep a person using a familiar solution after conditions change.

61
00:03:51.946 --> 00:03:54.985
Language uses symbols and rules to communicate meaning.

62
00:03:54.985 --> 00:03:58.412
Intelligence is measured in multiple ways and remains debated.

63
00:03:58.412 --> 00:04:00.512
Test reliability concerns consistency.

64
00:04:00.512 --> 00:04:03.662
Validity concerns whether a test measures what it claims.

65
00:04:03.662 --> 00:04:05.983
Standardization creates common procedures.

66
00:04:05.983 --> 00:04:12.007
Opportunity, culture, health, stress, and testing familiarity affect scores, so interpretation requires care.

67
00:04:12.007 --> 00:04:14.715
Memory involves encoding, storage, and retrieval.

68
00:04:14.715 --> 00:04:17.478
Sensory memory briefly holds incoming information.

69
00:04:17.478 --> 00:04:21.015
Working memory actively handles a limited amount of information.

70
00:04:21.015 --> 00:04:24.110
Long-term memory stores information over longer periods.

71
00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.255
Explicit memory includes facts and events that can be consciously recalled.

72
00:04:28.255 --> 00:04:33.394
Implicit memory influences behavior without conscious recollection, as with practiced skills.

73
00:04:33.394 --> 00:04:34.942
Attention supports encoding.

74
00:04:34.942 --> 00:04:38.092
A message never attended to is unlikely to be remembered.

75
00:04:38.092 --> 00:04:42.347
Elaborative rehearsal connects new material with meaning and prior knowledge.

76
00:04:42.347 --> 00:04:46.934
Retrieval practice strengthens access by requiring a person to produce information.

77
00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:50.803
Spacing practice over time usually works better than one long session.

78
00:04:50.803 --> 00:04:53.234
Recognition identifies familiar information.

79
00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:55.169
Recall produces it with fewer cues.

80
00:04:55.169 --> 00:05:00.143
Forgetting can result from encoding failure, decay, interference, or retrieval difficulty.

81
00:05:00.143 --> 00:05:04.177
Proactive interference occurs when old information disrupts new learning.

82
00:05:04.177 --> 00:05:08.543
Retroactive interference occurs when newer learning disrupts older information.

83
00:05:08.543 --> 00:05:11.361
Memory is reconstructive, not a flawless recording.

84
00:05:11.361 --> 00:05:18.269
Suggestive wording and later information can alter reports, which matters in interviews, complaints, and eyewitness accounts.

85
00:05:18.269 --> 00:05:23.630
Social psychology studies how people's thoughts, feelings, and behavior are influenced by others.

86
00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:26.504
Attribution theory examines how we explain behavior.

87
00:05:26.504 --> 00:05:32.859
The fundamental attribution error overemphasizes personal traits and underestimates situations when judging others.

88
00:05:32.859 --> 00:05:40.375
An employee may label a resident careless for missing paperwork while overlooking confusing instructions or an unstable mailing address.

89
00:05:40.375 --> 00:05:43.857
Attitudes have cognitive, emotional, and behavioral components.

90
00:05:43.857 --> 00:05:47.836
Cognitive dissonance is discomfort from inconsistent beliefs or actions.

91
00:05:47.836 --> 00:05:52.092
Persuasion depends on the source, message, audience, and depth of processing.

92
00:05:52.092 --> 00:05:55.021
Conformity aligns behavior or judgments with a group.

93
00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:57.066
Normative influence seeks acceptance.

94
00:05:57.066 --> 00:05:59.829
Informational influence treats others as evidence.

95
00:05:59.829 --> 00:06:03.366
Compliance follows a request, while obedience follows authority.

96
00:06:03.366 --> 00:06:05.245
Groupthink can suppress criticism.

97
00:06:05.245 --> 00:06:09.113
Group polarization can move discussion toward a more extreme position.

98
00:06:09.113 --> 00:06:13.866
The bystander effect can reduce helping when responsibility is spread among observers.

99
00:06:13.866 --> 00:06:19.061
Prejudice is an attitude, stereotypes are generalized beliefs, and discrimination is behavior.

100
00:06:19.061 --> 00:06:21.382
In-groups and out-groups shape perception.

101
00:06:21.382 --> 00:06:28.677
Contact can reduce prejudice under supportive conditions such as equal status, shared goals, cooperation, and institutional backing.

102
00:06:28.677 --> 00:06:33.872
Social psychology therefore informs both public communication and the design of participation.

103
00:06:33.872 --> 00:06:36.125
Apply these ideas to the county reminder.

104
00:06:36.125 --> 00:06:38.984
First, define the outcome before looking at results.

105
00:06:38.984 --> 00:06:44.095
Use completed appointment within the scheduled window, not a convenient measure chosen later.

106
00:06:44.095 --> 00:06:46.843
Second, compare like cases during the same period.

107
00:06:46.843 --> 00:06:51.625
If lawful and ethical, randomly assign eligible appointments to the old or new message.

108
00:06:51.625 --> 00:06:55.473
Keep timing, language availability, and follow-up procedures constant.

109
00:06:55.473 --> 00:06:59.210
Analyze all assigned cases, including messages that fail to deliver.

110
00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.628
The independent variable is message version.

111
00:07:01.628 --> 00:07:03.662
The dependent variable is completion.

112
00:07:03.662 --> 00:07:08.993
Delivery status, case type, lead time, language, and prior contact history are relevant measures.

113
00:07:08.993 --> 00:07:17.458
Random assignment supports causal inference, but it does not repair a broken outcome definition or an intervention that excludes residents without phones.

114
00:07:17.458 --> 00:07:21.580
Learning principles can improve the service without manipulating residents.

115
00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:24.053
Use a consistent sender and recognizable cue.

116
00:07:24.053 --> 00:07:25.812
State the needed action clearly.

117
00:07:25.812 --> 00:07:28.286
Give immediate confirmation after completion.

118
00:07:28.286 --> 00:07:32.518
Avoid punishment language that increases anxiety without clarifying behavior.

119
00:07:32.518 --> 00:07:37.629
If the task has several steps, shape progress with a checklist and confirmation at each step.

120
00:07:37.629 --> 00:07:44.005
Cognition and memory suggest short sentences, one primary action, and information placed near the time it is needed.

121
00:07:44.005 --> 00:07:48.017
A reminder three weeks early may support planning but still be forgotten.

122
00:07:48.017 --> 00:07:51.095
A second reminder closer to the date supports retrieval.

123
00:07:51.095 --> 00:07:58.240
Include an easy way to reschedule, because remembering is not the same as having control over work, transportation, or caregiving.

124
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:00.604
Social psychology improves feedback design.

125
00:08:00.604 --> 00:08:04.561
Do not show residents a running approval tally before asking their view.

126
00:08:04.561 --> 00:08:07.144
Avoid leading questions from authority figures.

127
00:08:07.144 --> 00:08:10.717
Invite people who missed appointments, not only successful users.

128
00:08:10.717 --> 00:08:16.763
Let staff privately record concerns before a group meeting so the first speaker does not anchor everyone else.

129
00:08:16.763 --> 00:08:19.786
Publish unfavorable findings along with favorable ones.

130
00:08:19.786 --> 00:08:24.898
Suppose completion is eighty percent with the new text and seventy percent with the old text.

131
00:08:24.898 --> 00:08:31.163
Report the size of the difference, group counts, uncertainty, delivery failures, and any departures from the plan.

132
00:08:31.163 --> 00:08:31.988
Then replicate.

133
00:08:31.988 --> 00:08:39.188
One study can guide a decision, but a durable civic practice requires evidence that survives another period and another population.

134
00:08:39.188 --> 00:08:40.418
Three recall questions.

135
00:08:40.418 --> 00:08:44.376
First, what features distinguish an experiment from a correlational study?

136
00:08:44.376 --> 00:08:48.227
Second, how do positive reinforcement and negative reinforcement differ?

137
00:08:48.227 --> 00:08:51.917
Third, why can retrieval practice improve memory more than rereading?

138
00:08:51.917 --> 00:09:00.387
First, an experiment manipulates an independent variable, measures a dependent variable, and uses a comparison design, ideally with random assignment.

139
00:09:00.387 --> 00:09:06.937
A correlational study measures association without controlled manipulation and cannot by itself establish causation.

140
00:09:06.937 --> 00:09:08.688
Second, both increase behavior.

141
00:09:08.688 --> 00:09:11.624
Positive reinforcement adds a desirable consequence.

142
00:09:11.624 --> 00:09:14.843
Negative reinforcement removes an unpleasant consequence.

143
00:09:14.843 --> 00:09:22.635
Third, retrieval practice makes the learner actively produce information, strengthening access routes and revealing what is not yet known.

144
00:09:22.635 --> 00:09:25.854
Rereading can create familiarity without reliable recall.

145
00:09:25.854 --> 00:09:29.708
Artifact: Draft a one-page test plan for the county reminder.

146
00:09:29.708 --> 00:09:43.608
Define the hypothesis, independent and dependent variables, assignment method, ethical safeguards, memory design, feedback method, and the exact sentence you would use if the result were correlational rather than causal.

147
